+# Decision: Realtime Delivery Architecture
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard distributes committed stall-status changes from one authoritative board per event to as many as 800 simultaneous staff browsers. A busy market normally produces about two changes per minute, with opening-time bursts reaching 20 edits per second. Connected browsers must receive committed changes within 15 seconds, browsers disconnected for up to 20 minutes must recover the latest board, and connectivity failures must remain distinguishable from an unchanged board.
+
+The delivery architecture must preserve server-assigned event revisions while coordinating three components: the change store, the delivery service, and browser synchronization. Five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, and a hosted realtime service can all satisfy the formal freshness target. The choice therefore turns on routine latency, provider independence, operating cost, and the operational work MarketBoard is prepared to own.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Realtime-provider independence (major).** Board delivery should not depend on a provider-specific publication or subscription model when MarketBoard can operate the capability at its expected scale.
+- **Routine update latency (major).** Changes should normally appear promptly enough for coordinators to work from a shared live board, even though the formal limit remains 15 seconds.
+- **Ongoing delivery cost (moderate).** The design should avoid repeated reads or service fees that grow unnecessarily with connected-browser time.
+- **Operational burden (moderate).** Connection capacity, failure handling, and recovery mechanisms consume engineering and operating effort; reducing that burden is valuable but does not override the major criteria.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+**Description.** Each browser periodically requests changes after its last known revision. The server remains stateless with respect to browser connections, and the change store serves cursor-based reads. Standard request/response infrastructure handles delivery, while the browser owns the polling loop and catch-up cursor.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- **Strong fit for realtime-provider independence.** The application uses its own HTTPS and storage infrastructure without a realtime intermediary.
+- **Weak fit for routine update latency.** A committed change routinely waits as long as the polling interval before the next request begins.
+- **Weak fit for ongoing delivery cost.** Every connected browser generates repeated reads even when the board has not changed.
+- **Strong fit for operational burden.** Stateless requests avoid persistent-connection capacity and reconnect-session management.
+
+**Summary.** Polling minimizes connection operations at the cost of routine lag and continuous read traffic.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** MarketBoard operates persistent WebSocket connections and publishes committed event revisions to subscribed browsers. The change store retains replayable revisions and supports reliable reconciliation between commit and publication. Delivery manages subscriptions, fanout, connection capacity, and session termination. Browsers track the last applied revision, reconnect after interruption, and catch up before resuming live updates.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- **Strong fit for realtime-provider independence.** The wire protocol and subscription behavior remain under MarketBoard's control and do not depend on a realtime vendor.
+- **Strong fit for routine update latency.** The server can push a committed revision without waiting for a browser request interval.
+- **Strong fit for ongoing delivery cost.** Delivery consumes self-hosted connection capacity but avoids idle polling reads and recurring realtime-service fees.
+- **Weak fit for operational burden.** MarketBoard must size persistent connections and handle disconnects, reconnects, stale sessions, replay, and commit-to-publication failures.
+
+**Summary.** Self-hosted sockets trade additional connection and recovery operations for low latency and control of the delivery path.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+**Description.** MarketBoard publishes committed changes through a managed realtime provider, and browsers use the provider's subscription client. The provider operates persistent connection capacity and much of the reconnect transport. MarketBoard still reconciles durable event revisions with provider publication and preserves a recovery path when publication or subscription fails.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- **Weak fit for realtime-provider independence.** Publication, credentials, client subscriptions, and operational behavior become coupled to the selected provider.
+- **Strong fit for routine update latency.** Managed push delivery can distribute committed changes without a polling interval.
+- **Weak fit for ongoing delivery cost.** The service introduces recurring fees tied to provider pricing and usage.
+- **Strong fit for operational burden.** The provider owns most connection capacity and transport-level reconnect behavior, reducing MarketBoard's connection operations.
+
+**Summary.** Hosted realtime minimizes connection operations while introducing recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+MarketBoard uses self-hosted WebSockets. This option satisfies both major criteria: it provides fast routine updates while keeping the realtime delivery protocol and operation independent of a vendor. It also avoids polling's repeated idle reads and a hosted service's recurring fees.
+
+The selection deliberately accepts a weak fit on operational burden. MarketBoard must operate connection capacity, expose connection health, recover interrupted browsers from their last applied revision, and reconcile durable commits with publication. Those obligations are divided among the change-store, delivery, and browser-sync design nodes. The authoritative-revision and visible-connectivity tenets continue to constrain those designs: WebSocket delivery cannot become the source of truth, and a broken connection cannot be presented as a board with no new changes.
+
+This decision does not make subsecond delivery a formal requirement. The existing 15-second connected-browser goal remains the measurable boundary; low routine latency is the benefit that justifies accepting the additional operational work.
